- Dockerfile and verifiable build plan generation (requires `output_directory`); the CLI owns image builds, builder selection, caching, and publishing
- Kubernetes deployment

## Container runtime updates

The default runtime is the Core Python companion, pinned to its multi-platform
index digest. This agent selects Python companion 0.0.7, built with Python
3.14.10 on Alpine 3.24, and links Core 0.19.13. The companion is published
separately from the agent; a new release does not change existing service images.

Use `codefly update workspace` to adopt released agents, then rebuild and
publish the affected services with the CLI. A `docker-image` override keeps
its explicit selection and must be refreshed by its owner. Re-scan the resulting
service images: a fresh base does not remove vulnerabilities in application
dependencies or prove acceptance by a deployment's vulnerability and secret policies.

## What a native run approves

A native `codefly run` delivers your service to the host as the build it is
running, and this agent reports that build rather than letting the run guess
one. It names the interpreter `uv run` executes your app with — asked of uv, so
a `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT` or a `.python-version` of yours is honoured — along
with `pyproject.toml`, the `uv.lock` uv resolves against (your uv workspace
root's, when your project is a member of one) and every file under `src/`, not
only the `*.py` ones: a prompt or a schema your app loads is part of what runs.

The interpreter's own content is part of the build, so upgrading it — a
`uv python upgrade`, a new patch release, a different machine's python — is a
new approved build for a tree you did not edit. That is deliberate: your code
runs on that interpreter, and a run that approved the old one would be
approving something else.

Your virtual environment is not part of it. `.venv/` is uv's materialization of
the lock, and the lock and the interpreter already identify it; tool caches
(`__pycache__`, `.ruff_cache`, …) are derived from code already named, and
`tests/` is never imported by the serving process. Two things are therefore
outside the build: a `[tool.uv.sources]` path dependency's own code, whose
declaration is named but whose contents are not, and a container run, which
approves its image digest instead.

## Declared container health

Kubernetes probes follow the health predicates of the authored HTTP endpoint (`api: rest` or `api: http`) and transport. HTTP checks preserve accepted statuses and bounded body containment; redirects are not followed. Secured container-local checks authenticate the listener against the exact certificate mounted at `UVICORN_SSL_CERTFILE` before sending HTTP data. The isolated Python probe imports no application code and emits no response content or certificate material.

Startup, readiness and liveness are separate policies. The current Core Kubernetes manifest profile requires all three, so deployment refuses absent startup/liveness declarations instead of copying readiness into a restart policy. New scaffolds explicitly declare `/ready` readiness and transport startup/liveness — `/ready`, not `/version`, because every other route is admitted and a kubelet carries no admission; it answers 200 only while the replica holds the authority's published keys, so a replica that cannot admit a call withdraws from rotation rather than refusing every call sent to it. Existing services must author their own policies. Whole-second timing is required by Kubernetes; predicates or additional listener health that this agent cannot preserve are refused rather than silently omitted.

Image SBOM requests must provide immutable subjects and their registry/daemon source. The Core scanner preserves each requested identity; there is no registry-to-daemon fallback for a different image.
